<p>If you are unsure between heart rate and pace, the rule is simple: pace does not know if you slept poorly, if it is hot, or if you are stressed. Heart rate knows. For a first marathon, training by internal effort (pulse) usually protects the athlete from pushing the pace on days when the body needs recovery.</p>

<h2>The exact distribution of a week</h2>

<p>To illustrate how these concepts apply in practice, this is the cross-section of a classic week in the middle of a marathon build block for an athlete training 5 to 6 days:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Monday:</strong> Absolute rest from running. Heavy strength session in the gym.</li>
  <li><strong>Tuesday:</strong> Quality 1. Threshold work or intervals. The goal is high-end aerobic stimulus and maintaining running mechanics at speed.</li>
  <li><strong>Wednesday:</strong> Long easy run (zone 2) + strides at the end for neuromuscular work with no metabolic cost.</li>
  <li><strong>Thursday:</strong> Quality 2. Specific pace session or sustained tempo run.</li>
  <li><strong>Friday:</strong> Very easy and short run + Secondary strength session.</li>
  <li><strong>Saturday:</strong> Rest or 30 extremely easy minutes just to promote blood flow.</li>
  <li><strong>Sunday:</strong> Long Run. The third quality session. Over the block, this workout transitions from a simple long run to complex blocks (e.g., 32 km / 20 miles total, with the last 12 km / 7.5 miles exactly at race pace).</li>
</ul>

<p>If fatigue spirals out of control with this format, the variable you cut first is the Wednesday volume, never the Tuesday quality or the Sunday long run.</p>

<h2>The survival argument</h2>

<p>When we present this structure and this rigor with zones and volume, the counterargument is always the same: "I have a friend who ran the Chicago Marathon training three times a week, without stepping in the gym and without measuring any zones."</p>

<p>That is absolutely true. The human body is an impressive adaptation machine and is perfectly capable of surviving a lot of abuse. You can cross the finish line of a marathon with a bad plan, or with no plan at all.</p>

<p>What you cannot do is run the entire race. You cannot maintain your mechanical posture past kilometer 30 (mile 18). You cannot avoid systemic collapse at kilometer 35 (mile 21), and you definitely cannot walk downstairs facing forward the following Monday.</p>

<p>Finishing the race crawling or walking the last 10 kilometers (6 miles) is not the goal of a structured training program. Training for a marathon is not a blind survival test; it is a physiological engineering project. Build your base respecting the load steps. Respect your mechanical volume ceiling. Do the strength work and keep your easy runs in the correct zone. Race day is simply the mathematical execution of what you built and absorbed over the previous four months.</p>
